     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · BBC Business · 2026-10-22 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Barclays to Cut 5,000 Jobs Amid Increased AI Adoption</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Banking</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Job Cuts</span> <span>#Barclays</span></div>
       <p>Barclays is set to reduce its global workforce by approximately 5,000 staff, representing around 5% of its total employees, as the banking group increases its reliance on artificial intelligence (AI) within its operational processes. Of these cuts, 1,000 posts have already been closed, with a further 4,000 expected to be completed by the end of this year.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/barclays-to-cut-5000-jobs-amid-increased-ai-adoption.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The job reductions are primarily focused on the bank's UK retail banking arm, although its investment banking division is also affected. A significant proportion of these cuts are anticipated to impact back-office functions located in India, which support the UK operations. Barclays described the integration of AI as the &quot;icing on the cake&quot; of its ongoing modernisation plan, aimed at simplifying and improving its business.</p><p>Barclays Group chief executive CS Venkatakrishnan stated that the job cuts are a direct consequence of the significant investments the bank is making in technology. He emphasized AI's growing role, noting, &quot;AI has an important role to play and may have a more important role to play in the future. We will employ the AI at scale... But AI is the icing on the cake not the cake. Because you need to do the infrastructure build to get the benefits of AI.&quot; Approximately 60% of the job cuts are slated for support functions, including developer roles.</p><p>While not all reductions are solely attributable to AI, their scale intensifies concerns regarding the technology's broader impact on employment. Other financial institutions, including HSBC, Standard Chartered, and Danske Bank, have also indicated AI-related job cuts this year. Russ Mould, investment director at AJ Bell, commented that Barclays is &quot;reaching for the AI lever,&quot; even as its financial results slightly missed forecasts, with the bank reporting £2.3bn profit in the three months to September, higher than the previous year.</p><p>The bank is concluding a three-year cost-cutting programme and anticipates its efficiency measures will yield an additional £1.1bn in savings during 2027. Will Howlett, a financials analyst at Quilter Cheviot, remarked that these cuts illustrate &quot;how significantly AI and automation could reshape the banking industry over the next few years,&quot; highlighting that investment in technology is now translating into fewer personnel requirements.</p><p>What to watch: The broader banking sector's adoption of AI and its impact on workforce size.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly covers the BBC Business report, accurately citing figures, quotes, and expert commentary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
