     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-22 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Doorscaping Trend May Encourage Front Garden Planting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The social media trend of 'doorscaping' involves decorating front entrances with seasonal wreaths and foliage. Experts suggest this interest in aesthetic appeal could motivate homeowners to extend their gardening efforts to the space immediately below their doors.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/doorscaping-trend-may-encourage-front-garden-planting.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>British front yards are increasingly paved, with 55% of space currently covered, often due to parking needs or perceived size constraints. The Royal Horticultural Society recommends using containers and climbing plants as manageable, inexpensive starting points for those who lack the time or confidence for full garden maintenance.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures the doorscaping trend and RHS statistics from the source within proper brevity.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-autumn-wreaths-doorscaping-life-front.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
