     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-22 · editor 10/10 · 4 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Global Warming Halted Emerging Ice Age, New Study Suggests</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Global Warming</span> <span>#Ice Age</span> <span>#Glaciation</span> <span>#Climate Change</span></div>
       <p>Global warming may have effectively halted the onset of a new glacial period, or ice age, according to a recent study published in Science. Two thousand years ago, the Arctic remnants of what was once the vast Laurentide Ice Sheet began to expand again, marking the initial phase of a new glaciation. However, by the 1960s, summer temperatures had risen significantly, causing the ice sheet to start shrinking, thereby reversing the emerging ice age.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/global-warming-halted-emerging-ice-age-new-study-suggests.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nicolás Young, a geochemist at the Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory, part of the Columbia Climate School, stated, &quot;It was just getting started, everything was normal, the next glaciation was about to happen—and yet it stopped.&quot; He added, &quot;It's a very powerful example of how we have jolted the system.&quot; The research, conducted by Young, fellow Lamont geochemist Joerg Schaefer, and their colleagues, focused on the Laurentide Ice Sheet, which historically covered much of Canada and the northern United States between approximately 95,000 and 20,000 years ago.</p><p>At its peak, the Laurentide Ice Sheet stretched from above the Arctic Circle down to Illinois, with ice thicknesses exceeding two miles (3.2 kilometers) in some areas. The last remaining vestige of this colossal ice sheet is the Barnes Ice Cap, a Delaware-sized ice formation located on central Baffin Island in the Canadian territory of Nunavut. Young clarified, &quot;People always say that it disappeared, but that's not true.&quot; The team sought to determine if the Laurentide had ever contracted to a size smaller than its current state and subsequently regrown.</p><p>For clarity, while the term &quot;ice age&quot; is colloquially used to refer to glacial periods, Earth is technically still within the Late Cenozoic Ice Age, which commenced about 34 million years ago. This broader ice age encompasses alternating colder glacial periods and warmer interglacial periods, such as the one currently experienced. The last major glacial period concluded approximately 11,700 years ago.</p><p>Reconstructing the history of glaciers, particularly periods of maximum contraction and initial re-expansion, presents significant challenges due to the scouring effect of regrowing ice. However, the Barnes Ice Cap offers a unique advantage: it is frozen directly to its underlying bedrock and does not slide. This characteristic preserves vegetation that grew during earlier, smaller phases of the glacier and was subsequently entombed by its expansion.</p><p>By sampling vegetation exposed since the glacier's retreat began in the 1960s, scientists can determine its age using carbon-14 analysis. Young explained, &quot;If you do this enough times, you can actually develop a timeline of that ice margin systematically advancing over plants. You're actually tracking the expansion of that ice margin.&quot; Through this method, combining analyses from the Barnes Ice Cap and smaller melted caps, researchers established that the Laurentide reached its minimum size about 3,000 years ago before starting to re-expand approximately 2,000 years ago as the climate cooled sufficiently for ice accumulation.</p><p>This re-expansion 2,000 years ago, the researchers argue, should be considered the commencement of Earth's next glacial period. This interpretation challenges prevailing model-based studies that suggested the next glaciation was thousands or even tens of thousands of years away, asserting that the initial renewed growth of an ice sheet is the definitive starting point. The findings also provide validation for climate scientists in the 1970s who controversially claimed a new ice age was imminent. Young noted, &quot;It actually was happening,&quot; adding, &quot;They couldn't quite prove it, but now we sort of have the evidence.&quot;</p><p>Though it would have taken thousands of years for the cooling to become noticeable and around 30,000 years for the Laurentide to regain its continent-spanning mass, the next glaciation had indeed begun. However, by the 1960s, the Earth's climate had warmed to such an extent that the Barnes Ice Cap lost more ice during summer than it accumulated in winter, causing the emerging glaciation to &quot;fizzle out.&quot; Models predict the Barnes Ice Cap will melt entirely within the next few centuries, marking &quot;the true very last remains of the Laurentide, disappearing for good,&quot; Young warned.</p><p>Its disappearance could fundamentally alter the dynamics of future glaciations and ice ages. The Laurentide previously acted as a crucial glacial nucleation point, fostering self-sustaining feedbacks that promoted further ice growth when the climate cooled. In a world with elevated greenhouse gas concentrations, scientists are uncertain when the next glaciation might occur, and if continental ice sheets do begin to grow again, they may have to do so without the last surviving remnant of the Laurentide. Young concluded, &quot;What has been an extremely natural and predictable phenomenon is now gone.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: The continued monitoring of Arctic ice sheets and the implications for long-term climate predictions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive and accurate account of the study findings, incorporating all key scientific context and researcher quotes.</p>
