     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-22 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Laika CEO Travis Knight Denounces Generative AI</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Laika CEO Travis Knight has criticized generative AI as antithetical to his studio’s mission, emphasizing that human experience is essential to storytelling. While Laika utilizes advanced technology like 3D printing and sophisticated puppet mechanics for its new film 'Wildwood,' Knight distinguishes these tools from generative AI, which he describes as lacking soul and spirit.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/laika-ceo-travis-knight-denounces-generative-ai.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>'Wildwood' is a sprawling fantasy adventure five years in the making, featuring over 230 puppets and 49 animators. Knight argues that true art requires human emotion, noting that computers cannot experience love, loss, or grief. He maintains that technology only holds value when placed in the hands of an artist, rather than replacing them.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately captures the essence of the source, balancing technical production details with the CEO's perspective on AI.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/1016647/wildwood-travis-knight-interview-ai-laika-stop-motion" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
