       <h1>Spotify Defends Family Plan Verification as Routine Improvement</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Spotify has introduced a QR code verification method for its Family Plan to ensure all members reside in the same household. Users must scan a code provided by the plan manager within seven days to confirm their status, or risk losing access to the plan for 12 months.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/spotify-defends-family-plan-verification-as-routine-improvem.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During an investor call, co-CEO Alex Norström rejected claims that the move was a crackdown on account sharing. Instead, he characterized the update as a routine, ongoing improvement to existing security tools. The company aims to prevent users from bypassing subscription rules, maintaining the profitability of its Premium plans against unauthorized sharing.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures the essence of the Spotify Family Plan QR verification story without omissions of key points.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/22/spotify-confirms-family-plan-crackdown-but-calls-it-a-routine-improvement/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
