     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-22 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tesla Full Self-Driving Mode Implicated in Fatal I-80 Collision</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tesla</span> <span>#Full Self-Driving</span> <span>#FSD</span> <span>#Accident</span></div>
       <p>A 71-year-old man was fatally struck by a Tesla operating in Full Self-Driving (FSD) mode while crossing Interstate 80 in Vallejo, California. The incident occurred around 2:30 AM on Thursday, as reported by the San Francisco Chronicle. The California Highway Patrol (CHP) confirmed the collision in a statement to media outlets, indicating that their review of video footage from the Tesla's dashcam provided details of the moments leading up to the impact.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/tesla-full-self-driving-mode-implicated-in-fatal-i-80-collis.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The dashcam video, according to the CHP, &quot;showed the Tesla's brakes being applied as the vehicle steered to the left in an effort to avoid the pedestrian.&quot; Despite these evasive maneuvers, the right front portion of the Tesla ultimately struck the individual. Authorities believe that at least one other vehicle subsequently struck the pedestrian after the initial collision with the Tesla. However, only the driver of the Tesla stopped at the scene and remained there, while no other drivers involved in the secondary impacts stopped.</p><p>The CHP Solano Area office has issued a public appeal, urging anyone who witnessed the accident or possesses any relevant information to contact them as their investigation continues. This fatal incident follows a separate, related event on I-80 just last week.</p><p>In that prior incident, a driver claimed to have been asleep while using FSD mode when his Tesla collided with a police car that was stopped at the scene of an unrelated accident. Following this previous crash, the California Highway Patrol issued a stern warning to drivers. The CHP emphasized that &quot;Despite its name, full self-driving is not fully autonomous. Technology does not relieve drivers of their responsibility to safely operate their vehicles.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing investigations into FSD-related accidents and potential regulatory responses.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively summarizes the accident report and the context provided by the California Highway Patrol.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1016928/tesla-fsd-pedestrian-fatal-accident-california" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-22/tesla-full-self-driving-mode-implicated-in-fatal-i-80-collis" data-title="Tesla Full Self-Driving Mode Implicated in Fatal I-80 Collision"><b>Share:</b>
