     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-10-22 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UK Government Launches 'Be Ready' Campaign Advising Emergency Stockpiling</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The UK government has launched the 'Be Ready' campaign, urging citizens to prepare for 72 hours of self-sufficiency. Households are advised to store three days of food, nine litres of water per person, and essential supplies including blankets, cash, a first aid kit, and a battery-powered radio. The guidance aims to improve national resilience against climate change, cyberattacks, and hostile state threats.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-22/uk-government-launches-be-ready-campaign-advising-emergency-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Security minister Dan Jarvis stated the advice is for everyday emergencies, while defence secretary Wes Streeting highlighted risks from state-sponsored terrorism. This initiative aligns the UK with similar preparedness strategies in France, Ireland, and the Netherlands ahead of the winter storm season.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the government guidance and the context provided in the source while maintaining a concise tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/oct/22/britain-prepare-infrastructure-attacks-hostile-states-wes-streeting" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
